            <ArticleTitle>Reconstruction of advanced periacetabular metastatic lesions with modified Harrington procedure.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>178-85</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3944/AOTT.2015.15.0076</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The purpose of this study was to assess the outcomes of patients who had been treated by a modified Harrington procedure for advanced periacetabular metastases.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Between 2006 and 2013, 16 patients with a mean age of 57 years (range: 28-73 years) were treated using a modified Harrington technique. Extensive (class II/III) periacetabular defects were due to metastatic carcinoma or multiple myeloma. Surgical procedure included total hip replacement and acetabular reconstruction using threaded pins, cemented acetabular reinforcement ring, and/or polyethylene cup.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">All patients improved in regard to pain and walking ability. Mean preoperative and postoperative Musculoskeletal Tumour Society (MSTS) functional scores were 52.8% (range: 33.3-73.3%) and 72% (range: 56.6-90%), respectively (p&lt;0.05). There were 5 (31%) early or late complications requiring additional surgery. Implant survival was 75% and 37.5% at 12 and 18 months, respectively. Mean survival of the patients was 21 months (range: &lt;1-6 years). Six remained alive, with a mean survival of 27 months (range: 18 months to 5 years).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">This modified Harrington procedure can be used for reconstruction of advanced periacetabular metastatic lesions.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
